<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-adding" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Adding and Subtracting Fractions</title>

  <p> When adding or subtracting fractions with the same denominator (a common denominator), the numerators can be added or subtracted; 
  <md>\frac{a}{c}\pm\frac{b}{c}=\frac{a\pm b}{c}.</md></p>

  <example>
    <title>Adding and Subtracting Fractions with a Common Denominator</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate <m>\frac{5}{7}+\frac{4}{7}</m> and <m>\frac{5}{7}-\frac{4}{7}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Since these fractions have the same denominator, we can add the numerators.
        The denominator remains the same, so 
         <md>\frac{5}{7}+\frac{4}{7}=\frac{5+4}{7}=\frac{9}{7}.</md>
        Similarly, 
         <md>\frac{5}{7}-\frac{4}{7}=\frac{5-4}{7}=\frac{1}{7}.</md>
      </p>
    
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise label="ww-fraction-addition">
    <title>Adding Fractions with a Common Denominator</title>
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $ans = Fraction(13,9);
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate the following expression. 
                Provide your answer as a reduced fraction.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{8}{9} + \frac{5}{9} = </m> <var name="$ans" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                Because the denominators are identical, add the numerators together while keeping the denominator the same:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{8}{9} + \frac{5}{9} = \frac{8 + 5}{9} = \frac{13}{9}</m>
            </p>
        </solution> -->
    </webwork>
</exercise>

<p>What if the fractions have different numbers in the denominator? Then we first need to write the fractions
    in a different (but equivalent) form so that they have a common denominator. A typical choice of common denominator
    is the least common multiplier (LCM) of the two denominators, but an easy choice to make is to use their product. We multiply
    both the numerator and denominator of each fraction by the other denominator;

    <md>
        \frac{a}{c}\pm\frac{b}{d}=\frac{ad}{cd}\pm\frac{bc}{dc}.
    </md>
    Since <m>cd=dc</m>, these fractions have a common denominator, and so we can add or subtract as in the example above.
</p>

<example>
    <title>Adding and Subtracting Fractions with Different Denominators</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate <m>\frac{5}{8}+\frac{4}{7}</m> and <m>\frac{5}{8}-\frac{4}{7}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        These fractions have different denominators, 7 and 8. We first write them over a common denominator, <m>7\times 8=56</m>,
         <md>\frac{5}{8}+\frac{4}{7}=\frac{5\times 7}{8\times 7}+\frac{4\times 8}{7\times 8}=\frac{35}{56}+\frac{32}{56}.</md>
         Now they have the same denominator, and so we can add the numerators,
         <md>\frac{35}{56}+\frac{32}{56}=\frac{35+32}{56}=\frac{67}{56}.</md>
        Similarly, 
         <md>\frac{5}{8}-\frac{4}{7}=\frac{35}{56}-\frac{32}{56}=\frac{35-32}{56}=\frac{3}{56}.</md>
      </p>
    
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise label="ww-fraction-addition2">
    <title>Subtracting Fractions with Different Denominators</title>
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $ans = Fraction(8,45);
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate the following expression. 
                Provide your answer as a reduced fraction.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{7}{9} - \frac{3}{5} = </m> <var name="$ans" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>


<example>
    <title>Using the LCM</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate <m>\frac{3}{4}+\frac{5}{8}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        These fractions have different denominators, 4 and 8. We could use a common denominator of <m>4\times 8=32</m>, but
        we can instead use any common multiple of 4 and 8. The LCM of 4 and 8 is 8, and so we write the fractions over a common denominator of 8.</p>
        <p>This requires multiplying the numerator and denominator of the first fraction by 2,
         <md>\frac{3}{4}+\frac{5}{8}=\frac{3\times 2}{4\times 2}+\frac{5}{8}=\frac{6}{8}+\frac{5}{8}.</md>
         Now they have the same denominator, and so we can add the numerators,
         <md>\frac{6}{8}+\frac{5}{8}=\frac{6+5}{8}=\frac{11}{8}.</md>
      </p>
    
    </solution>
  </example>

